the hunt for the sixth person infected  with the brazilian variant of the virus  is moving  at a rapid pace such is the urgency of  identifying the person who forgot to put  their name and dress on the test  paperwork  that public health teams have even tried  tracing the kit  back through the postal system detective  work that's paying off  we've identified the batch of home test  kits in question  our search has narrowed from the whole  country down to  379 households in the southeast of  england and we're contacting each one  we're grateful that a number of  potential cases have come forward  following the call that we put out over  the weekend  so just how concerned should we be about  the p1  variant the first comprehensive study of  how the virus overwhelmed the city of  manaus and brazil  gives some clues first it spreads  more easily researchers from the uk and  brazil estimate that the variant is  between  1.4 and 2 times more transmissible  than previous versions of the virus  circulating in the city  second it evades the immune system for  every 100 people who develop covid19 in  the first wave last spring  between 25 and 61 were likely to be  re-infected third it's not  yet clear whether it causes more serious  infection  people who develop covid19 after the p1  variant started spreading in manaus  were between 10 and 80 percent more  likely to die  but the researchers can't be sure  whether this was because the virus was  more lethal  or because people struggled to get  medical care hospitals  ran out of oxygen because of demand the  key  question is what this means for the uk  for starters the scientists say just six  cases are unlikely to be enough to spark  an outbreak and secondly the p1 variant  may not have a  competitive edge over other viruses in  the uk  it has the same n501y mutation  as the kent variant both spread fast but  the brazilian variant shouldn't  overtake the existing dominant version  of the virus  now vaccines haven't yet been tested  against the brazilian virus  but scientists say that shouldn't slow  the roll out in the uk  bringing cases down to a low numbers  will give the test and trace system  a much better chance of identifying  people infected  with the new versions of the virus
 

